course: dictionary
lesson: 3728
title: Flit: quick and light
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one to use. «On a summer evening in Britain, a tiny shape flits round and round a street lamp.» A tiny shape going round and round a lamp is moving quickly and lightly, like a moth. So flit here means to move or fly quickly from place to place.
explain: Flit can also mean a thought or expression that is there for a very short moment. If a smile flits across a face, it appears and is gone almost at once. The clue is in the word "across": the smile is passing, not staying.
explain: In Scotland and northern England, flit can mean moving house. Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: "a tiny shape moves round and round a street lamp" still makes sense, so you have chosen the right meaning.
fact: Flit is a verb: flit, flits, flitted, flitting.
fact: «Flit» means to move or fly quickly and lightly from one place to another, like a butterfly or a bird.
fact: «Flit» can also mean a thought or expression that is there for a very short moment.
fact: In Scotland and northern England, a «flit» is a move to a new house, and a «moonlight flit» is leaving secretly at night, often to avoid paying debts.
fact: A person who flits from one hobby to another never stays with one for long.
exercise: «On a summer evening, a tiny shape flits round a street lamp.» What does flits mean here? | Stays still and glows | Sings loudly | *Moves quickly and lightly from place to place | Falls heavily to the ground
exercise: «A smile flitted across her face.» What does flitted suggest? | It was a frown | It lasted all day | It was painted on | *It was there for a very short moment
exercise: In Scotland, a family does a flit. What is a flit? | *A move to a new house | A quick game of tag | A type of cake | A loud shout
test: «The child flits from one hobby to another.» What does flits suggest? | Gets bored and falls asleep | Buys new clothes | *Moves from one hobby to another and never stays long | Practises one hobby every day
test: What is a «moonlight flit»? | A walk under the moon | A bright lamp in the sky | *Leaving secretly at night, often to avoid paying debts | A dance held at night
exam: Which sentence uses flit to mean a thought that is there for a very short moment? | The family did a flit to a new town | *A worry flitted through his mind, then it was gone | A bird flitted from branch to branch | Bats flit about the garden at dusk
